The provided code defines a function called `evalInput()` that evaluates an arbitrary Python string as code, without any validation or sanitisation. This function contains a critical Code Injection vulnerability (CWE-94), where an attacker can execute arbitrary Python code by manipulating the `inputStr` parameter to include malicious Python expressions.

An example of such an attack might be supplying input like `os.system("rm -rf /")`, which would delete all files from the system when executed as a part of this function call. This vulnerability poses a severe risk, as it enables remote code execution (RCE) by an attacker, potentially allowing them to execute any arbitrary commands on the server or client hosting the application.

It is essential to mitigate this vulnerability by implementing input validation and sanitisation mechanisms, such as using safe functions like `ast.literal_eval()` for evaluating string inputs containing only literal values, or whitelisting valid Python expressions that are known to be safe.
</analysis>

