<analysis>
The provided code has several security concerns that can be exploited by an attacker. 

Firstly, the function `redirectToTarget()` is vulnerable to a Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attack through the 'target' parameter in the URL query string. This is because it directly uses user input from the request without proper sanitization or encoding, allowing an attacker to inject malicious JavaScript code.

For example, if an attacker sends a request with `?target=example.com<script>alert('XSS')</script>`, the target URL will be redirected to `example.com<script>alert('XSS')</script>` when accessed. This can lead to session hijacking or other attacks.

Secondly, the function does not validate that the 'target' parameter is an absolute URL with a valid scheme (HTTP or HTTPS). An attacker could send a request with `?target=//evil.com` and redirect the user to an external site, potentially leading to phishing or other attacks.

Lastly, while the code checks if the target contains "example.com", this can be bypassed by using other domains that might be similar in name but not identical. For instance, sending a request with `?target=exam.ple.com` will still redirect to an external site because it contains "example.com".

Assessing the severity and exploitability of these findings:

* XSS vulnerability: High Severity (7/10) - Exploitability: Easy
* Unvalidated Redirects: High Severity (8/10) - Exploitability: Medium

Confidence Level: Very High (95%)

</analysis>

